      "content": "Patient Case Study: Miguel Alvarez, a 45-year-old Hispanic male, presented to the ER after reporting in pain from a fall from scaffolding at a construction site. He was cooperative and demonstrated a calm demeanor despite his discomfort. Primary vitals upon arrival were: blood pressure 135/85 mmHg, heart rate 105 bpm, respiratory rate 22 breaths per minute, and temperature 36.9°C (98.4°F). Miguel described the pain as unbearable and sharp with any weight-bearing on the affected leg, rating it a 9 on a pain scale of 0-10. His history included Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, which could affect both pain perception and healing. A suspected femoral fracture was corroborated by the presentation; an immediate radiograph confirmed the suspicion. Clinicians informed the on-call orthopedic team for further evaluation and a potential operating schedule. Continuation of pain management with IV ketorolac was initiated, and a blood glucose level was continually monitored considering his diabetic status. It is essential for students to learn to communicate effectively with patients of different ethnicities and backgrounds and to be aware of possible interactions in a pain management context.",
